# Line endings are decided **here**, not by each developer's `core.autocrlf` (419).
#
# The failure that made this file necessary: a `perl -0pi` edit with `\n` patterns silently did nothing on a CRLF working tree, three times, and the
# edit "succeeded". Whether a file is CRLF in the working tree is therefore not a detail of someone's git config — it is a property of the
# repository, and this is where it is written down.
#
# `text=auto` normalises to LF **in the index** and checks out with the platform convention. Nothing changes for an existing clone; what changes is
# that a machine with `core.autocrlf=false` no longer stores CRLF, so the same edit stops behaving differently on two machines.
* text=auto

# Source and text: LF in the repository.
*.rs       text
*.toml     text
*.md       text
*.sql      text
*.json     text
*.yml      text
*.yaml     text
*.txt      text

# Shell scripts must stay LF even on a Windows checkout: a CRLF shebang line makes bash answer `bad interpreter`.
*.sh       text eol=lf

# Binary: never normalised, never diffed as text.
*.dll      binary
*.exe      binary
*.sqlite   binary
*.pack     binary
*.png      binary

# Test baselines under `scripts/` are compared byte-for-byte; normalising them would silently change what a baseline means.
scripts/baselines/** -text
